course: dictionary
lesson: 15019
title: Respectively
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: When a sentence names two or more things and then gives two or more results, respectively tells you that each result goes with the thing in the same place. «Tom and Sue have 5 and 8 apples respectively.» The first number goes with the first name, so Tom has 5 and Sue has 8.
explain: Think of two lines of people standing side by side. The first person in one line is matched with the first person in the other, the second with the second, and so on. «The pens, paints and crayons are in the red, blue and green boxes respectively.» Pens go in red, paints in blue and crayons in green.
explain: Respectively is an adverb, so it tells you how things are matched: in order. It is common in maths and science, where you must match each answer to its label. Check by reading the two lists side by side and matching them in order.
fact: Respectively is an adverb that means in the same order as the things just mentioned.
fact: «Tom and Sue have 5 and 8 apples respectively» means Tom has 5 and Sue has 8.
fact: Respectively is used when two lists match up one to one, in the same order.
fact: «The cats, dogs and birds are fed at 7, 8 and 9 respectively» means the cats are fed at 7, the dogs at 8 and the birds at 9.
fact: A useful check is to count the items in each list. If the numbers match, you can pair them in order.
exercise: «Ali and Maya scored 12 and 15 points respectively.» Who scored 15 points? | Both scored 12 | *Maya | Ali | Nobody scored 15
exercise: «The bags, hats and coats were on the chairs, table and bed respectively.» Where were the hats? | On the chairs | Under the bed | *On the table | On the bed
exercise: «Sam, Priya and Leo ran 3, 5 and 4 laps respectively.» How many laps did Priya run? | 3 | 4 | *5 | 12
test: «Paul and Ruth are 9 and 10 years old respectively.» How old is Ruth? | 9 | 19 | Paul | *10
test: «The lamps, rugs and clocks cost £5, £12 and £20 respectively.» Which item costs £12? | *Rugs | Clocks | Lamps | £20
exam: What does respectively mean? | *In the same order as the things just mentioned | All at the same time | Very quickly, one after another | Mostly, but not always
